{
  "$schema": "node_modules/wrangler/config-schema.json",

  // Matches the Cloudflare Workers Builds project, which is named after the
  // connected GitHub repo (kurdim12/thinkqulity-design — the misspelling is
  // the repo's, and the project inherited it). CI overrides any other value and
  // warns that it will open a PR to reconcile, so keep this in step by hand.
  // The public origin is therefore https://thinkqulity-design.<subdomain>.workers.dev
  "name": "thinkqulity-design",
  "main": ".open-next/worker.js",

  // Derived the way the adapter derives it (dist/cli/utils/create-wrangler-config.js
  // -> getLatestCompatDate): it fetches the LATEST PUBLISHED `workerd` from the
  // npm registry and reads the date out of its `major.yyyymmdd.patch` version,
  // capped at today.
  //
  // MIND THE GAP. That is the registry's latest, NOT the workerd this lockfile
  // pins. The `workerd` actually INSTALLED here is 1.20260811.1
  // (node_modules/workerd/package.json) — a transitive dependency, pinned to
  // that exact version by miniflare 5.20260811.1-alpha and wrangler 4.123.0
  // (`npm ls workerd`); it is not a dependency of this project. Its npm wrapper
  // advertises `compatibilityDate` = "2026-08-11" (node_modules/workerd/lib/
  // main.js), which is what `require('workerd').compatibilityDate` returns.
  //
  // The BINARY's ceiling is later than that. Run directly with a config asking
  // for 2026-08-14 it loads with no date complaint; asked for 2026-09-01 it
  // refuses with 'This Worker requires compatibility date "2026-09-01", but the
  // newest date supported by this server binary is "2026-08-18".' (observed
  // 2026-08-15 by running node_modules/@cloudflare/workerd-windows-64/bin/
  // workerd.exe against a one-line worker). So TODAY there is no gap:
  // `opennextjs-cloudflare preview` — which is `wrangler dev` -> miniflare ->
  // that binary — runs the same 2026-08-14 semantics production gets, and the
  // local check DEPLOY.md §5 relies on is telling the truth.
  //
  // WHAT HAPPENS IF THEY DIVERGE, i.e. if this date is ever moved past the
  // installed binary's ceiling (2026-08-18 for 1.20260811.1): preview does not
  // clamp and carry on. workerd rejects the worker's config with the error
  // quoted above and exits instead of serving. Miniflare separately rejects any
  // date after today's UTC date (ERR_FUTURE_COMPATIBILITY_DATE), and workerd
  // does the same ("Can't set compatibility date in the future"). wrangler
  // still carries a handler for an older fallback warning, matched on the
  // prefix "The latest compatibility date supported by" (wrangler-dist/cli.js,
  // WranglerLog.warn) — but nothing in this tree emits that message any more,
  // so do not expect a silent fallback. Deploying is unaffected either way:
  // Cloudflare's edge runs a current workerd.
  //
  // Closing a real gap means updating `wrangler`, which brings its pinned
  // miniflare and workerd with it — NOT editing the date below down to match an
  // older local binary, which would move production backwards to fix a
  // local-only mismatch.
  //
  // It must stay at or after 2024-09-23 regardless: that is the date from which
  // `nodejs_compat` means full Node.js compatibility rather than the old partial
  // polyfill set (miniflare's NODEJS_COMPAT_V2_SWITCH_OVER_DATE). OpenNext runs
  // the Next.js server as Node.js code.
  "compatibility_date": "2026-08-14",
  "compatibility_flags": [
    // Required by OpenNext. The bundled Next.js server imports `node:*` builtins
    // (async_hooks, buffer, stream, crypto); without Node compatibility the
    // Worker fails while loading its modules, which returns no useful
    // diagnostics at all.
    //
    // At a compatibility_date of 2026-08-04 or later the flag is ON BY DEFAULT
    // (miniflare's NODEJS_COMPAT_DEFAULT_ON_DATE), so at the date above this
    // line is redundant and is kept as an explicit statement, not because
    // loading would fail without it. Two consequences worth knowing: the raw
    // workerd binary treats a redundant flag as a config ERROR ("The
    // compatibility flag nodejs_compat became the default as of 2026-08-04 so
    // does not need to be specified anymore" — observed on 1.20260811.1), and
    // miniflare strips redundant nodejs_compat flags before handing the config
    // over (stripRedundantNodejsCompatFlags in miniflare/dist/src/index.js), so
    // preview never sends it. Whether the upload API is equally strict on
    // deploy was not verified from this tree; `wrangler deploy --dry-run` does
    // not exercise it.
    "nodejs_compat",

    // Required. Makes every outbound fetch() take the real public-internet path
    // instead of Workers' default same-zone shortcut, so requests to Supabase,
    // OpenRouter and Apify behave like they do under `next start`. Without it,
    // a fetch to a hostname that resolves onto Cloudflare can loop back into
    // this same Worker instead of leaving it.
    "global_fetch_strictly_public"
  ],

  "assets": {
    // Both values are produced by `opennextjs-cloudflare build`; they are the
    // adapter's fixed output paths, not preferences.
    "directory": ".open-next/assets",
    "binding": "ASSETS"
  },

  // ===================================================================== //
  // DO NOT DELETE THIS LINE. It is load-bearing and looks like boilerplate.
  //
  // Workers bound CPU time, not wall clock — time spent waiting on Supabase,
  // OpenRouter or Apify does not count. But the PAID (Standard) default ceiling
  // is 30_000 ms, and this app declares far more work than that: seven routes
  // set `maxDuration = 300` (audience, board/analyze, comments, compliance,
  // generate/[feature], monitor, profile), one sets 120 (refresh) and one sets
  // 60 (ingest) — nine routes above the 30s default.
  //
  // Note that `maxDuration` itself is INERT here: it is a Vercel construct and
  // nothing on Workers reads it (see the comment at src/lib/agent/client.ts:15).
  // This `cpu_ms` value is the only ceiling that actually applies, so deleting
  // it silently reinstates 30s and those nine routes start dying mid-request.
  //
  // 300000 ms = 300 s, matching the longest declared route budget.
  // ===================================================================== //
  "limits": { "cpu_ms": 300000 },

  // Pin execution beside the database. Supabase for this app is in EU-Central
  // (Frankfurt) and EVERY route is database-bound: `next build` renders all 44
  // entries in its route table as `ƒ (Dynamic)` — zero static, zero SSG. Of the
  // 43 route/page modules under src/app, 30 say so outright with
  // `export const dynamic = 'force-dynamic'` (all 28 route.ts, plus login and
  // auth/callback); the other 13 page.tsx are dynamic by inference, because the
  // `(app)` layout reads cookies through `getOperator()` and src/app/page.tsx
  // redirects. Either way nothing is served from cache, and every request makes
  // at least one Supabase round trip, usually several in sequence.
  //
  // Without the hint, placement optimises for proximity to the VIEWER, which for
  // this app means Amman. That would put the Worker one continent away from the
  // data and turn each sequential query into a transcontinental round trip —
  // i.e. "edge" placement makes this specific app SLOWER, not faster.
  //
  // The hint is a Cloudflare REGION GROUP, not a cloud-provider region. The
  // deploy API rejects anything else — the first CI deploy on 2026-08-15 failed
  // with error 10021: `invalid placement hint 'aws:eu-central-1'. Valid options
  // are: wnam, enam, sam, weur, eeur, apac, oc, afr, me`. Frankfurt is `weur`.
  // (wrangler's schema types the hint as a bare string, so a wrong value passes
  // local validation and only fails at the API. Verify against that list.)
  "placement": { "mode": "smart", "hint": "weur" },

  // Workers Logs. Needed to see a 1102 (isolate out of memory) or an exception
  // after the fact; without it a failed request leaves no trace to read.
  "observability": { "enabled": true }

  // ------------------------------------------------------------------ //
  // ENVIRONMENT BINDINGS — deliberately absent from this file.
  //
  // src/lib/env.ts is the authority on the key set (the `EnvKey` union and the
  // total `ENV_SPECS` record classify every key as required/optional and as
  // secret/var). Read it there; do not maintain a second list here.
  //
  // Nothing is written here because no key in that table has a value that is
  // both non-secret and known:
  //   - the credentials (SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY, OPENROUTER_API_KEY,
  //     ANTHROPIC_API_KEY, OPENAI_API_KEY, APIFY_TOKEN) and ALLOWED_EMAILS must
  //     never enter a tracked file — bind them with `wrangler secret put NAME`;
  //   - NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL and NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY are
  //     public-by-design (they are inlined into the client bundle regardless),
  //     so a `vars` block is an honest home for them; `wrangler secret put`
  //     lands on process.env identically if you prefer to keep them out of git.
  //     DEPLOY.md §2 covers both. Nothing is pre-filled here — the operator
  //     chooses;
  //   - every remaining var (AI_PROVIDER, AI_MODEL_*, APIFY_ACTOR,
  //     APIFY_PROFILES, APIFY_BUDGET_USD, COMMENTS_TOP_N, COMMENTS_PER_POST,
  //     MIRROR_MEDIA, IG_HANDLE_*, EMBEDDING_*) is an OVERRIDE with a documented
  //     default in code. Writing the defaults out here would duplicate them and
  //     let the two drift. Add a `vars` block only to override one, and only
  //     with a value the operator actually chose — APIFY_BUDGET_USD in
  //     particular has no honest default and must not be guessed.
  //
  // Locally, `opennextjs-cloudflare preview` reads `.dev.vars` (gitignored).
  // `GET /api/health` reports which bindings are actually present at runtime.
  //
  // Two things that bite, both consequences of how the adapter builds:
  //   1. `next build` runs with the AMBIENT environment, not with these
  //      bindings. src/lib/supabase/browser.ts:12-13 reads
  //      process.env.NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_{URL,ANON_KEY} as literal property
  //      accesses, which Next inlines into the CLIENT bundle at build time.
  //      Those two must therefore be present when `cf:build` runs, not only on
  //      the deployed Worker.
  //   2. Conversely, the adapter compiles `.env*` files into the worker bundle
  //      (dist/cli/build/open-next/compile-env-files.js -> .open-next/cloudflare/
  //      next-env.mjs, imported by the worker). Building with a populated
  //      .env.local bakes those values, secrets included, into .open-next/ and
  //      from there into what `deploy` uploads. `npm run cf:build`
  //      (scripts/cf-build.mjs) exists to prevent exactly that: it leaves only
  //      the NEXT_PUBLIC_* entries visible to the adapter for the duration of
  //      the build and fails on any leak it can detect. Running
  //      `opennextjs-cloudflare build` directly bypasses it. .open-next/ is
  //      gitignored — keep it that way, and see DEPLOY.md §2 and §5.
  // ------------------------------------------------------------------ //

  // ------------------------------------------------------------------ //
  // OMITTED FROM THE ADAPTER'S TEMPLATE, each for a checked reason:
  //
  //   "r2_buckets" (NEXT_INC_CACHE_R2_BUCKET) and the WORKER_SELF_REFERENCE
  //   "services" entry — both exist to serve the incremental cache and its
  //   revalidation queue. There is no ISR/SSG output in this app to cache: every
  //   entry in the build's route table is `ƒ (Dynamic)` (see the placement note
  //   above for how the 43 modules get there) and not one of them declares
  //   `export const revalidate` — the whole tree has zero occurrences of it.
  //   See open-next.config.ts, where incrementalCache is commented out to match.
  //
  //   "images" (the IMAGES binding) — enables the next/image optimizer. This app
  //   does not use next/image: the only remote images are Supabase Storage URLs
  //   rendered through a plain <img> (src/app/(app)/brand/page.tsx:370-374), and
  //   next.config.ts configures no `images.remotePatterns`.
  //
  // Restore the relevant block if either assumption stops holding.
  // ------------------------------------------------------------------ //
}
